{"id":21,"role":"User","content":"Your previous turn produced reasoning but no response before stopping. Read the reasoning below and CONTINUE from where it ends, producing the tool call(s) or final answer you were about to write. Do not repeat the reasoning; just resume and finish.\n\n--- captured reasoning ---\nNow I have a comprehensive understanding of the situation. Let me think carefully about the strategy.\n\nThe key obstructions are:\n1. θ⁻⁰ depends on both x and y (interface links u⁰ are shared)\n2. Pushforward of μ⁻ is singular (point mass at σ(u⁰))\n3. σ reflection gives χ(g)² not |χ(g)|²\n\nThe OS proof resolves this via T = B*·B. The design doc §5 suggests the \"derived axiom\" approach:\n1. Axiomatize the separable decomposition as a derived axiom\n2. Prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from it (pure measure theory + character orthogonality, count stays 6)\n3. Prove the derived axiom from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette (count → 5)\n\nLet me think about what the derived axiom should look like. The key mathematical fact is:\n\nAfter the change of variables (step b), the integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) ·\n    exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nThe interface Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))` is a product of interface plaquette factors. By `plaquette_product_separable_decomp`, this decomposes as:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w F(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n```\nwhere the product is over all links in interface plaquettes, and F(w) ≥ 0.\n\nNow, the links in interface plaquettes split into three groups:\n- L⁺: links from U⁺ (positive links appearing in interface plaquettes)\n- L⁰: links from u⁰ (interface links)\n- L_V: links from V⁺ (positive links representing reflected negative links)\n\nSo we can write:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺)\n```\nwhere:\n- Φ_w(U⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ L⁺} χ_{w(l)}(U⁺_l)\n- Ψ_w(u⁰) = ∏_{l ∈ L⁰} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)\n- Ξ_w(V⁺) = ∏_{l ∈ L_V} χ_{w(l)}(V⁺_l)\n\nNow, by the reflection symmetry S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺)):\n```\n∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺)\n```\n\nThis means the character expansion is symmetric under the swap. But this doesn't immediately tell us that Φ_w = Ξ_w.\n\nHmm, actually the issue is more subtle. The character expansion via `plaquette_product_separable_decomp` treats all links uniformly — it doesn't distinguish between U⁺ links, u⁰ links, and V⁺ links. The decomposition is:\n```\n∑_w F(w) · ∏_{l ∈ L_all} χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n```\nwhere L_all = L⁺ ∪ L⁰ ∪ L_V and g_l is the link variable at link l.\n\nThe reflection symmetry says that if we swap the roles of U⁺ and V⁺ (and apply σ to u⁰), the interface action is unchanged. This means the character expansion should be \"symmetric\" in some sense.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The key insight from the OS proof is:\n\nThe transfer matrix T has kernel:\n```\nK_T(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n```\n\nWait, actually let me re-read the transferMatrixReflected definition more carefully.\n\nFrom the code:\n```\ntransferMatrixReflected (ψ : PosInterfaceConfig N T L → ℝ) (u : PosInterfaceConfig N T L) : ℝ :=\n  let S_plus := osPositiveOfPosInterface N T L β u\n  ∫ (V_plus : ...),\n    ψ (mergePosInterface N T L V_plus (sigmaInterface N T L (restrictToInterface N T L u))) *\n    Real.exp (-β * (S_plus / 2 +\n      osPositiveOfPosInterface N T L β\n        (mergePosInterface N T L V_plus (sigmaInterface N T L (restrictToInterface N T L u))) / 2 +\n      wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β\n        (extendToFullConfig N T L (reflectPosToNeg N T L V_plus) u)))\n    ∂ haarMeasurePositive N T L\n```\n\nSo `(Tψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)`\n\nAnd the integral we want to show ≥ 0 is:\n```\nI = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n  = ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nNow, `g(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)` (from the definition of g_posInterface). Wait, let me check.\n\nActually, let me look at the definition of g_posInterface and integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg.\n\nLet me think about the structure. We have:\n- g(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)  (the \"g\" function)\n- (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nWait, but g already includes the exp(-β·S⁺/2) factor. So:\n- g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2)\n\nSo (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n= exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me look at the actual definitions.\n\nActually, I think the key point is that the integral I = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) needs to be shown ≥ 0.\n\nLet me think about the operator-theoretic approach. The idea is:\n1. The interface Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) decomposes via character expansion.\n2. This decomposition, combined with the S⁺ factors, gives a kernel K(u, V⁺) that can be written as ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) or similar.\n3. The integral I then becomes ∑_w a_w · |∫ g · Φ_w|² ≥ 0.\n\nBut the obstructions say this doesn't work directly because of the shared u⁰ and the σ reflection.\n\nLet me think about the \"derived axiom\" approach more concretely. \n\nThe derived axiom would state something like: \"The transfer matrix T is a positive operator,\" or more precisely, \"The integral ∫_u g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) ≥ 0 for all g.\"\n\nBut that's exactly what transferMatrixPositivity_axiom says (after the equivalence via integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg). So the derived axiom would need to be more specific.\n\nActually, let me re-read the design doc §5 more carefully:\n\n\"1. Axiomatize the separable decomposition of the interface Boltzmann factor as a 'derived axiom' (a consequence of Peter-Weyl).\n2. Prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from the derived axiom (pure measure theory + character orthogonality). This removes transferMatrixPositivity_axiom, replacing it with the derived axiom (count stays at 6).\n3. Prove the derived axiom from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette (pure combinatorics/representation theory). This removes the derived axiom (count → 5).\"\n\nSo the derived axiom is about the separable decomposition of the interface Boltzmann factor. Let me think about what form it should take.\n\nThe interface Boltzmann factor is:\n```\nK_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))\n```\n\nBy `plaquette_product_separable_decomp`, this decomposes as:\n```\nK_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n```\n\nThe links l in interface plaquettes come from three sources: U⁺, u⁰, and V⁺ (= reflect(U⁻)). So:\n```\nK_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺)\n```\n\nNow, the key question is: can we use the reflection symmetry to show that this has the form ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺)))?\n\nActually, I think the right way to think about this is:\n\nThe full kernel of the transfer matrix (including the S⁺ factors) is:\n```\nK_T(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · K_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺)\n```\n\nAnd the integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K_T(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nWait, no. Let me re-derive. We have:\n- g(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)\n- (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nSo:\n- g(u) · (Tg)(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n= f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n= f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nHmm, this is getting messy. Let me look at the actual definitions in the code.\n\nActually, I think I should focus on the strategy rather than getting bogged down in the details. Let me think about what's the most tractable approach.\n\nThe design doc §5 suggests the \"derived axiom\" approach. Let me think about what the derived axiom should be.\n\nOption A: The derived axiom states that the interface Boltzmann factor has a specific separable decomposition.\n\nOption B: The derived axiom states that the transfer matrix kernel has a specific form that makes it a positive operator.\n\nI think Option A is cleaner. The derived axiom would be:\n\n\"For the interface Boltzmann factor K_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))), there exists a finite index set W, non-negative coefficients a : W → ℝ, and functions Φ : W → (PosInterfaceConfig → ℂ) such that:\n```\nK_int(U⁺, u⁰, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))\n```\n\"\n\nThen step 2 would use this decomposition + character orthogonality to show the integral is ≥ 0.\n\nBut wait, the obstructions say that even with this decomposition, the integral doesn't factor into |Fourier coefficients|² because of the shared u⁰ and σ reflection.\n\nHmm, let me think again. The obstruction 3 says:\n```\nI = ∑_i a_i · ∫_{u⁰} A_i(u⁰) · conj(A_i(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\nwhere A_i(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_i(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺).\n\nThis is ⟨A_i, A_i ∘ σ⟩, which is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nBut the OS proof says T = B*·B, which gives ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0. So the operator B must be defined in a way that accounts for the σ reflection.\n\nLet me think about what B is. In the OS proof, B is defined via the character expansion. The key is that the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor, combined with the S⁺ factors, gives a kernel that can be written as:\n\nK_T(u, v) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))\n\nwhere u, v are both PosInterfaceConfigs. Then T is the integral operator with this kernel, and T = B*·B where B extracts the Fourier coefficients.\n\nBut the issue is that the kernel K_T(u, v) involves the reflection map, so it's not obviously of the form ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. After the change of variables, the transfer matrix is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nThe kernel of T (as an operator on L²(μ⁺⁰)) is:\n```\nK_T(u, v) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(v⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(v⁺)))) · [Jacobian]\n```\n\nWait, no. The kernel of T as an integral operator is:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫_v K_T(u, v) · ψ(v) dμ⁺⁰(v)\n```\n\nBut in our case, (Tψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ... dμ⁺(V⁺). The argument of ψ is (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), which is a PosInterfaceConfig. So v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), and the integral is over V⁺ with v⁰ = σ(u⁰) fixed.\n\nSo the kernel is:\n```\nK_T(u, v) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(v)/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(v⁺)))) \n```\n\nwhere v = (v⁺, v⁰) and the δ ensures v⁰ = σ(u⁰).\n\nThis is a \"degenerate\" kernel — it's supported on the submanifold v⁰ = σ(u⁰). This is obstruction 2 (the pushforward is singular).\n\nSo the kernel is NOT of the standard form ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)). The δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)) makes it degenerate.\n\nHmm, so how does the OS proof handle this? Let me think...\n\nThe OS proof doesn't work at the level of the kernel K_T. Instead, it works at the level of the operator. The key is:\n\n1. The interface Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S_int) decomposes via character expansion.\n2. This decomposition, combined with the S⁺ factors, defines an operator B.\n3. T = B*·B.\n\nThe operator B is defined as follows. The character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor gives:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺)\n```\n\nwhere Φ_w depends on (U⁺, u⁰) and Ξ_w depends on V⁺. (The u⁰ dependence is in Φ_w because interface links appear in the plaquettes.)\n\nWait, but the interface links u⁰ appear in BOTH the original and reflected plaquettes. So the decomposition might not cleanly separate into (U⁺, u⁰)-dependent and V⁺-dependent parts.\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The interface plaquettes are of three types:\n- Type A: involve U⁺ and u⁰ links (no V⁺ links)\n- Type B: involve V⁺ and u⁰ links (no U⁺ links) — after change of variables\n- Type C: involve u⁰ links only (no U⁺ or V⁺ links)\n\nSo the interface Boltzmann factor factorizes as:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = [∏_{type A} exp(...)] · [∏_{type B} exp(...)] · [∏_{type C} exp(...)]\n```\n\n- Type A factors depend on (U⁺, u⁰) only\n- Type B factors depend on (V⁺, u⁰) only\n- Type C factors depend on u⁰ only\n\nSo:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = K_A(U⁺, u⁰) · K_B(V⁺, u⁰) · K_C(u⁰)\n```\n\nNow, each of these can be character-expanded:\n- K_A(U⁺, u⁰) = ∑_w F_A(w) · Φ_w^A(U⁺) · Ψ_w^A(u⁰)\n- K_B(V⁺, u⁰) = ∑_w F_B(w) · Φ_w^B(V⁺) · Ψ_w^B(u⁰)\n- K_C(u⁰) = ∑_w F_C(w) · Ψ_w^C(u⁰)\n\nSo:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = ∑_{w_A, w_B, w_C} F_A(w_A)·F_B(w_B)·F_C(w_C) · Φ_{w_A}^A(U⁺) · Ψ_{w_A}^A(u⁰) · Φ_{w_B}^B(V⁺) · Ψ_{w_B}^B(u⁰) · Ψ_{w_C}^C(u⁰)\n```\n\nThe u⁰-dependent part is: Ψ_{w_A}^A(u⁰) · Ψ_{w_B}^B(u⁰) · Ψ_{w_C}^C(u⁰). This is a product of characters of the same group element (the interface link variables), which can be reduced via CG to a single character sum.\n\nSo after CG reduction:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w^A(U⁺) · Φ_w^B(V⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰)\n```\n\nwhere G(w) ≥ 0 and Ψ_w(u⁰) is a single character (or sum of characters with non-negative coefficients).\n\nNow, by the reflection symmetry S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺)):\n```\n∑_w G(w) · Φ_w^A(U⁺) · Φ_w^B(V⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w^A(V⁺) · Φ_w^B(U⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))\n```\n\nThis means the decomposition is symmetric under swapping U⁺ ↔ V⁺ and u⁰ ↔ σ(u⁰). In particular, if we relabel, we get that Φ_w^A and Φ_w^B are related by the reflection.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the character expansion of the FULL kernel (including S⁺ factors) gives a decomposition of the form:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))\n= ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))\n```\n\nWait, but this is exactly the form that `character_expansion_positivity` requires! And the obstructions say it doesn't work because of the shared u⁰ and σ reflection.\n\nLet me re-read obstruction 3 more carefully.\n\nObstruction 3 says: even if we split the integral ∫_u = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} and apply the lemma for each fixed u⁰, the result is:\n```\nI = ∑_i a_i · ∫_{u⁰} A_i(u⁰) · conj(A_i(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\nwhich is ⟨A_i, A_i ∘ σ⟩, NOT ‖A_i‖².\n\nSo the issue is that the σ reflection on u⁰ means we get A_i(u⁰) · conj(A_i(σ(u⁰))) instead of A_i(u⁰) · conj(A_i(u⁰)) = |A_i(u⁰)|².\n\nBut the OS proof says T = B*·B. How does this resolve the issue?\n\nI think the key is that the operator B is defined in a way that \"absorbs\" the σ reflection. Specifically, B might be defined as:\n```\n(Bg)(w) = ∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\nand then B*·B has kernel:\n```\n(B*·B)(u, v) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))\n```\n\nBut this requires the kernel to be of the form ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)), which is the standard form. The issue is that our kernel has the σ reflection built in.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Maybe the right approach is:\n\n1. The interface Boltzmann factor decomposes as:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))\n```\n\nThis is the \"derived axiom\" — the separable decomposition with the σ reflection built in.\n\n2. Then the full kernel is:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))\n= ∑_w a_w · [exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰)] · conj([exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))])\n= ∑_w a_w · Ψ_w(u) · conj(Ψ_w(v))\n```\n\nwhere v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) and Ψ_w(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · Φ_w(u).\n\nWait, but this requires that exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) = exp(-β·S⁺(v)/2) where v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)). Is this true?\n\nv = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is a PosInterfaceConfig. S⁺(v) = S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)). Yes, this is exactly what appears in the kernel. So:\n\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Ψ_w(u) · conj(Ψ_w(v))\n```\n\nwhere v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) and Ψ_w(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · Φ_w(u).\n\nNow, the integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K_full(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(v) · ∑_w a_w · Ψ_w(u) · conj(Ψ_w(v)) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nwhere v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, the change of variables V⁺ → v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is NOT measure-preserving from μ⁺ to μ⁺⁰ because v⁰ = σ(u⁰) is fixed (deterministic given u⁰). This is obstruction 2.\n\nBut wait — we can split the integral differently. Let's write:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ∑_w a_w · Ψ_w(u⁺, u⁰) · conj(Ψ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nFor fixed u⁰, the inner double integral over (u⁺, V⁺) is:\n```\n∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ∑_w a_w · Ψ_w(u⁺, u⁰) · conj(Ψ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n= ∑_w a_w · [∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Ψ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)] · conj([∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Ψ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺)])\n= ∑_w a_w · A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰)))\n```\n\nwhere A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Ψ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺).\n\nThen:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∑_w a_w · A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w a_w · ⟨A_w, A_w ∘ σ⟩_{L²(μ⁰)}\n```\n\nThis is obstruction 3: ⟨A_w, A_w ∘ σ⟩ is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nSo the direct approach fails. The OS proof must use a different structure.\n\nLet me think about what the OS proof actually does. The key is that the operator B is NOT defined as (Bg)(w) = ∫ g · Ψ_w. Instead, B is defined in a way that accounts for the σ reflection.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor, when done correctly, gives a decomposition where the u⁰-dependent part is ALSO expanded in characters, and the σ reflection is handled by the dual map.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The interface Boltzmann factor is:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = K_A(U⁺, u⁰) · K_B(V⁺, u⁰) · K_C(u⁰)\n```\n\nEach factor is character-expanded:\n- K_A(U⁺, u⁰) = ∑_s a_s · Φ_s^A(U⁺) · Ψ_s^A(u⁰)\n- K_B(V⁺, u⁰) = ∑_t b_t · Φ_t^B(V⁺) · Ψ_t^B(u⁰)\n- K_C(u⁰) = ∑_r c_r · Ψ_r^C(u⁰)\n\nThe product is:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = ∑_{s,t,r} a_s b_t c_r · Φ_s^A(U⁺) · Φ_t^B(V⁺) · Ψ_s^A(u⁰) · Ψ_t^B(u⁰) · Ψ_r^C(u⁰)\n```\n\nNow, the u⁰-dependent part Ψ_s^A(u⁰) · Ψ_t^B(u⁰) · Ψ_r^C(u⁰) is a product of characters of the interface link variables. By CG, this reduces to:\n```\nΨ_s^A(u⁰) · Ψ_t^B(u⁰) · Ψ_r^C(u⁰) = ∑_w N^w_{s,t,r} · χ_w(u⁰)\n```\nwith N^w_{s,t,r} ≥ 0.\n\nSo:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = ∑_{s,t,r,w} a_s b_t c_r N^w_{s,t,r} · Φ_s^A(U⁺) · Φ_t^B(V⁺) · χ_w(u⁰)\n```\n\nNow, by the reflection symmetry, we also have:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int) = exp(-β·S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺)))\n= ∑_{s,t,r,w} a_s b_t c_r N^w_{s,t,r} · Φ_s^A(V⁺) · Φ_t^B(U⁺) · χ_w(σ(u⁰))\n```\n\nNow, χ_w(σ(u⁰)) = χ_w applied to the σ-reflected interface links. For time-like interface links, σ inverts them, so χ_w(σ(u⁰)) involves conj(χ) for time-like links. By the dual map, conj(χ_w) = χ_{dual(w)}.\n\nSo χ_w(σ(u⁰)) = χ_{σ*(w)}(u⁰) for some transformed index σ*(w) (involving dual maps for time-like links).\n\nThis is getting very complicated. Let me step back and think about the strategy.\n\nI think the most tractable approach is the \"derived axiom\" approach from §5, but I need to think carefully about what the derived axiom should be.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The key mathematical fact is that T = B*·B. The operator B is defined via the character expansion. The proof that T = B*·B uses:\n1. The character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor (step a, done).\n2. The change of variables (step b, done).\n3. Character orthogonality to evaluate the integrals (step c).\n4. Assembling to get T = B*·B (step d).\n\nThe difficulty is that the σ reflection on interface links means the decomposition is not of the standard form. But the OS proof handles this by defining B appropriately.\n\nLet me think about what B is concretely. In the OS proof, B is the operator that extracts Fourier coefficients. Specifically:\n\n(Bg)(w) = ∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nwhere Φ_w are the character basis functions. Then:\n\n(B*·B g)(u) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · (Bg)(w) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · ∫_v g(v) · conj(Φ_w(v)) dμ⁺⁰(v)\n\nSo the kernel of B*·B is:\nK_{B*B}(u, v) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))\n\nAnd T = B*·B means:\nK_T(u, v) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v))\n\nBut our kernel K_T is degenerate (supported on v⁰ = σ(u⁰)). So T ≠ B*·B in the standard sense.\n\nHmm, I think the issue is that I'm confusing two different things. Let me re-read the OS proof structure.\n\nActually, I think the OS proof works at a different level. The transfer matrix T is NOT a standard integral operator with a kernel K_T(u, v). Instead, T is defined as:\n```\n(Tψ)(u) = ∫_{V⁺} ψ(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nwhere K(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))).\n\nThe key is that T acts on functions of u = (U⁺, u⁰), but the integral is over V⁺ only (not over the full PosInterfaceConfig space). The σ(u⁰) in the argument of ψ means T is NOT a standard integral operator.\n\nTo show T is positive, we need to show ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0, i.e.:\n```\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) ≥ 0\n```\n\nSubstituting:\n```\n∫_u g(u) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u) ≥ 0\n```\n\nNow, the character expansion of K(u, V⁺) gives:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)))\n```\n\nWait, but this is the form that leads to obstruction 3. Let me think about whether the character expansion actually gives this form or a different form.\n\nActually, I think the character expansion gives:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(U⁺, u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺, u⁰)\n```\n\nwhere Φ_w depends 