rier coefficients|². We need to use character orthogonality.\n\nIf Ψ_λ(u⁰) = χ_{λ}(u⁰) (a single character), then:\n```\n∫_{u⁰} A_λ(u⁰) · χ_λ(u⁰) · A_λ(σ(u⁰)) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nThis is NOT character orthogonality. Character orthogonality would be ∫ χ_λ · conj(χ_μ) = δ_{λμ}. Here we have A_λ(u⁰) · A_λ(σ(u⁰)) · χ_λ(u⁰), which is a product of three functions.\n\nI think the issue is that A_λ(u⁰) is NOT a character. It's a general function of u⁰. So character orthogonality doesn't directly apply.\n\nHmm, but the OS proof says T = B*·B. Let me think about what B is in this context.\n\nActually, I think the key is that the operator B acts on the FULL PosInterfaceConfig space, not just on u⁺ or u⁰ separately. The character expansion of the FULL kernel (including S⁺ factors) gives:\n```\nK_full(u, v) = ∑_λ a_λ · Φ_λ(u) · conj(Φ_λ(v))\n```\n\nwhere u, v are both PosInterfaceConfigs, and Φ_λ(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · [character expansion of the interface part].\n\nBut this requires the kernel to be of the standard form, which it's NOT (because of the σ reflection).\n\nOK, I think I've been going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach and think about what's actually tractable for formalization.\n\nGiven the complexity of the full OS proof, I think the best strategy is:\n\n1. **Define a \"derived axiom\" that captures the key identity:**\n```\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∑_w a_w · |∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)|²\n```\nThis directly implies positivity.\n\n2. **Prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from this derived axiom** (pure measure theory, no character orthogonality needed — the identity IS the positivity).\n\n3. **Prove the derived axiom from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette + characterOrthogonality** (the hard part, but it's pure representation theory + measure theory).\n\nFor step 2, the derived axiom directly gives:\n```\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫_u g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∑_w a_w · |∫ g·Φ_w|² ≥ 0\n```\n\nFor step 3, we need to show that the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor + S⁺ factors, combined with character orthogonality, gives the identity.\n\nBut actually, I realize that the derived axiom as stated above is essentially just \"T is a positive operator,\" which is what we're trying to prove. The derived axiom should be more specific — it should capture the character expansion structure.\n\nLet me think about a more specific derived axiom. The key mathematical fact is:\n\n**The full kernel K_full(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)))) has a separable decomposition:**\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰)))\n```\n**where θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is the reflected config, a_w ≥ 0, and Φ_w are character basis functions.**\n\nIf this decomposition holds, then:\n```\nI = ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(θ(V⁺, u⁰)) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nNow, the change of variables v = θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) maps μ⁺ to... well, for fixed u⁰, V⁺ → (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is a map from positive configs to PosInterfaceConfigs. The pushforward of μ⁺ by this map is μ⁺ × δ_{σ(u⁰)} (a point mass on the interface part). This is NOT μ⁺⁰.\n\nSo the change of variables gives:\n```\nI = ∫_u ∫_{v: v⁰ = σ(u⁰)} g(u) · g(v) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)) d(μ⁺ × δ_{σ(u⁰)})(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u g(u) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺)) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u g(u) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(A_w(u⁰)) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nwhere A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺).\n\nThis is:\n```\nI = ∑_w a_w · ∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) · conj(A_w(u⁰)) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} conj(A_w(u⁰)) · ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} conj(A_w(u⁰)) · A_w(u⁰) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} |A_w(u⁰)|² dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w a_w · ‖A_w‖²_{L²(μ⁰)} ≥ 0\n```\n\nWait, this works! Let me check the steps more carefully.\n\nThe key step is:\n```\n∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺) = A_w(u⁰)\n```\n\nIs this true? We defined A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺). For this to equal ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺), we need:\n```\n∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n```\n\nThis would be true if the change of variables V⁺ → u⁺ with σ(u⁰) → u⁰ is measure-preserving and g, Φ_w are \"reflection-symmetric\" in the appropriate sense.\n\nActually, this is NOT generally true. A_w(u⁰) involves g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) and Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)), while the other integral involves g(u⁺, u⁰) and Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰). These are different unless σ(u⁰) = u⁰ (which is not true for time-like links).\n\nSo my calculation above is wrong. Let me redo it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The decomposition is:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰)))\n```\n\nwhere θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nThe integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(θ(V⁺, u⁰)) · K_full(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nNow, split u = (u⁺, u⁰):\n```\nI = ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nFor fixed u⁰, the inner integrals over (u⁺, V⁺) separate:\n```\n∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰))) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n= A_w(u⁰) · conj(B_w(u⁰))\n```\n\nwhere:\n- A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n- B_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nSo:\n```\nI = ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁰) · conj(B_w(u⁰)) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nNow, B_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺).\n\nBy the change of variables u⁰' = σ(u⁰) (which is measure-preserving on μ⁰ by the σ reflection being measure-preserving):\n```\nB_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nThis is a function of σ(u⁰). If we define C_w(u⁰') = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, u⁰') · Φ_w(V⁺, u⁰') dμ⁺(V⁺), then B_w(u⁰) = C_w(σ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺) = C_w(u⁰) (by relabeling u⁺ → V⁺).\n\nWait, is A_w(u⁰) = C_w(u⁰)? Let me check:\n- A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n- C_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(V⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nYes, these are the same (just relabeling u⁺ → V⁺). So A_w(u⁰) = C_w(u⁰).\n\nTherefore B_w(u⁰) = C_w(σ(u⁰)) = A_w(σ(u⁰)).\n\nSo:\n```\nI = ∑_w a_w · ∫_{u⁰} A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nThis is ⟨A_w, A_w ∘ σ⟩_{L²(μ⁰)}, which is obstruction 3 — NOT necessarily non-negative!\n\nSo the decomposition K_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰))) does NOT directly give positivity. The σ reflection causes the problem.\n\nThis confirms that the direct approach doesn't work. The OS proof must use a different structure.\n\nLet me think about what the OS proof actually does. I think the key is that the character expansion is done MORE carefully, so that the u⁰-dependent part is handled differently.\n\nSpecifically, I think the OS proof expands the FULL kernel (including S⁺ factors) in characters of ALL link variables (U⁺, u⁰, V⁺), and then uses character orthogonality to evaluate the integrals over ALL link variables, not just u⁰.\n\nLet me think about this. The full kernel is:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n```\n\nThis is a function of (U⁺, u⁰, V⁺). By the character expansion (applied to ALL plaquette factors, including the S⁺ plaquettes), we get:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n```\n\nwhere the product is over ALL links (U⁺ links, u⁰ links, V⁺ links, and also the spatial links in S⁺ plaquettes).\n\nWait, but the S⁺ plaquettes involve only U⁺ and u⁰ links (not V⁺ links). And the S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) plaquettes involve V⁺ and σ(u⁰) links. So the full kernel involves:\n- U⁺ links (from S⁺(u) and S_int)\n- u⁰ links (from S⁺(u), S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)), and S_int)\n- V⁺ links (from S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) and S_int)\n\nThe character expansion gives:\n```\nK_full(u, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺)\n```\n\nwhere Φ_w is the product over U⁺ links, Ψ_w over u⁰ links, Ξ_w over V⁺ links.\n\nNow, the integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} g(u⁺, u⁰) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(u⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nBut g(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2). The S⁺ factor is ALREADY included in K_full. So we have a double-counting issue.\n\nWait, no. Let me re-derive. The integral is:\n```\nI = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nwhere (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺).\n\nSo:\n```\nI = ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nNow, g(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2), so g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)).\n\nAnd g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2).\n\nSo:\n```\nI = ∫_u f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n```\n\nSo the kernel is:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n= exp(-β·(S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n```\n\nNote: S⁺(u) appears with coefficient 1 (not 1/2), and S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) appears with coefficient 1 (not 1/2). This is because g includes the exp(-β·S⁺/2) factor, and (Tg) includes another exp(-β·S⁺/2) factor.\n\nSo the full kernel is:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n```\n\nNow, S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) is the action of a \"two-slice\" system: the positive slice (U⁺, u⁰), the reflected positive slice (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), and the interface action between them.\n\nBy the reflection symmetry, this is symmetric under the swap (U⁺, u⁰) ↔ (V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, the character expansion of K(u, V⁺) gives:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰))\n```\n\nWait, the S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)