n\nSo the decomposition K(u, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))) is consistent with the reflection symmetry (it just says K is real).\n\nNow, if this decomposition holds, then:\n```\nI = ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))) · [∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺)] · conj([∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺)]) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nDefine A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺). Then:\n∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) = A_w(σ(u⁰)) [by relabeling V⁺ → u⁺ and σ(u⁰) → u⁰... wait, no. A_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺). And ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) = A_w(σ(u⁰)). Yes!]\n\nSo:\n```\nI = ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))) · A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} [A_w(u⁰) · Ψ_w(u⁰)] · conj([A_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))]) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n= ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} C_w(u⁰) · conj(C_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nwhere C_w(u⁰) = A_w(u⁰) · Ψ_w(u⁰).\n\nThis is ⟨C_w, C_w ∘ σ⟩_{L²(μ⁰)}, which is obstruction 3 — NOT necessarily non-negative!\n\nSo even with the decomposition K(u, V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))), the integral is NOT a sum of |Fourier coefficients|².\n\nThis confirms that the direct approach doesn't work. The σ reflection is the fundamental obstruction.\n\nSo how does the OS proof actually work? I think the key must be that the character expansion is done in a way that the u⁰-dependent part is handled by character orthogonality, NOT by the σ-reflection structure.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Maybe the key is that the character expansion of the FULL kernel (including S⁺ factors) gives a decomposition where the u⁰ links appear with characters that are EIGENFUNCTIONS of the σ reflection.\n\nSpecifically, if Ψ_w(u⁰) is a character χ_λ(u⁰), and the σ reflection maps χ_λ to χ_{σ*(λ)} (via the dual map for time-like links), then:\n```\n∫_{u⁰} χ_λ(u⁰) · conj(χ_{σ*(λ)}(u⁰)) · A_w(u⁰) · conj(A_w(σ(u⁰))) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nThis is still not character orthogonality because of the A_w factors.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) is a general function of u⁰ (because f is arbitrary). So no matter how we decompose the kernel, the u⁰ integral will involve A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰)), which is NOT a character.\n\nI think the OS proof must use a completely different approach. Let me search for the actual proof.\n\nActually, let me re-read the gap_analysis.md more carefully. It says:\n\n\"The OS proof resolves this by constructing B via the character expansion and showing T = B*·B.\"\n\nAnd the key steps are:\n1. Expand each interface plaquette factor in characters.\n2. For interface time-like links, the product χ_s(g) · conj(χ_t(g)) arises, decomposable via CG.\n3. For links in multiple plaquettes, CG reduces products to single characters.\n4. The resulting decomposition defines B, and T = B*·B.\n\nI think the key is step 4: \"The resulting decomposition defines B.\" The operator B is NOT defined as (Bg)(w) = ∫ g · Φ_w. Instead, B is defined via the character expansion of the interface Boltzmann factor, in a way that accounts for the σ reflection.\n\nLet me think about what B is. The interface Boltzmann factor is:\n```\nexp(-β·S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺)\n```\n\nThe operator B might be defined as:\n```\n(Bg)(w) = ∫_{U⁺} ∫_{u⁰} g(U⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) dμ⁺(U⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n```\n\nThen B*·B has kernel:\n```\nK_{B*B}(u, v) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(v⁰))\n```\n\nAnd T = B*·B means:\n```\n(Tg)(u) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · ∫_v g(v) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(v⁰)) dμ⁺⁰(v)\n```\n\nBut our T is:\n```\n(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n```\n\nFor T = B*·B, we need:\n```\nexp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(V⁺) = ∑_w F(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · ∫_v g(v) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(v⁰)) dμ⁺⁰(v)\n```\n\nThe RHS integrates over the FULL PosInterfaceConfig space (v⁺, v⁰). The LHS integrates over V⁺ only, with v⁰ = σ(u⁰).\n\nFor these to be equal, the RHS must also \"collapse\" to v⁰ = σ(u⁰). This would happen if conj(Ψ_w(v⁰)) is non-zero only when v⁰ = σ(u⁰), which is not the case for characters.\n\nI think the issue is that T is NOT equal to B*·B as operators. Instead, the QUADRATIC FORM ⟨g, Tg⟩ equals ‖Bg‖², but T ≠ B*·B.\n\nWait, that can't be right either. If ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² = ⟨g, B*·B g⟩ for all g, then T = B*·B (by polarization, since both are self-adjoint).\n\nHmm, but T might not be self-adjoint. Let me check.\n\nActually, the OS proof shows T is positive, which implies T is self-adjoint (for bounded operators on a Hilbert space). But the proof of positivity might not go through T = B*·B.\n\nOK, I think I've spent way too long on the mathematical analysis. Let me take a practical approach.\n\nThe design doc §5 suggests the \"derived axiom\" approach. Let me think about what derived axiom would be:\n1. Tractable to prove from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette + characterOrthogonality.\n2. Sufficient to prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom.\n\nThe most natural derived axiom is one that directly states the positivity of the transfer matrix, but in a more \"structured\" way that can be proved from the character expansion.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe the right approach is NOT the \"derived axiom\" approach, but rather to directly prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from the existing axioms.\n\nThe existing axioms are:\n1. peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette — provides the character expansion + CG + dual map.\n2. characterOrthogonality — provides Schur orthogonality.\n\nThe transferMatrixPositivity_axiom states:\n```\n∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0\n```\n\nBy the existing lemmas (osG_thetaG_factorization, integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg, transferMatrix_change_of_variables), this is equivalent to:\n```\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) ≥ 0\n```\n\nwhere (Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...