{"id":23,"role":"Assistant","content":"I've been doing extensive mathematical analysis. Let me now take a practical approach: search the literature for the actual OS proof structure (especially how the σ reflection is handled), and read the key code definitions to understand the exact formalization target.","timestamp":1785490721,"token_count":70,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"task_items\":[{\"content\":\"Study OS/Lüscher proof structure for handling σ reflection on interface links\",\"id\":\"1\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"in_progress\"},{\"content\":\"Read key code definitions (g_posInterface, transferMatrixCorrect, integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg, osG)\",\"id\":\"2\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"pending\"},{\"content\":\"Decide on formalization strategy (direct vs derived axiom) based on proof structure\",\"id\":\"3\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"pending\"},{\"content\":\"Implement step (c): character expansion + CG + character orthogonality\",\"id\":\"4\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"pending\"},{\"content\":\"Implement step (d): conclude T = B*·B and positivity\",\"id\":\"5\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"pending\"},{\"content\":\"Verify with #print axioms, update README/Overview/docs, lake build clean\",\"id\":\"6\",\"priority\":\"high\",\"status\":\"pending\"}],\"action\":\"update\"}","name":"todo_list"},"id":"5d7b56b0-ad44-41b8-b797-b5eec80959aa","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":8155,"reasoning_content":"Let me take stock of where I am. I've been doing extensive mathematical analysis trying to understand the OS proof structure. The key insight I've arrived at is:\n\n1. The direct `character_expansion_positivity` approach fails due to obstruction 3 (σ reflection gives ⟨A_w, A_w ∘ σ⟩ not ‖A_w‖²).\n\n2. The OS proof uses T = B*·B, but the exact form of B is subtle because of the degenerate kernel (v⁰ = σ(u⁰)).\n\n3. The \"derived axiom\" approach from §5 is the most tractable for formalization.\n\nI've been going in circles on the mathematical details. Let me take a completely practical approach now. Instead of trying to fully understand the OS proof, let me:\n\n1. Look at the actual code to understand the current state\n2. Think about what's the most tractable next step for formalization\n3. Start implementing\n\nThe key realization is that I should focus on what's actually doable in Lean, not on fully understanding the mathematics. The \"derived axiom\" approach is the right strategy because it decomposes the problem into cleaner pieces.\n\nLet me think about the derived axiom more carefully. The design doc §5 says:\n1. Axiomatize the separable decomposition as a derived axiom\n2. Prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from it (pure measure theory + character orthogonality, count stays 6)\n3. Prove the derived axiom from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette (count → 5)\n\nFor step 2, I need a derived axiom that implies positivity. The most natural one is:\n\n**Derived Axiom:** The transfer matrix kernel K(u, V⁺) has a separable decomposition of the form:\n```\nK(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰)))\n```\nwhere θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), a_w ≥ 0.\n\nBut as I showed, this leads to obstruction 3. So this CAN'T be the right derived axiom.\n\nWait, but actually, let me reconsider. The `character_expansion_positivity` lemma DOES work if we can apply it. The issue is the three obstructions. But maybe with the right formulation, we can work around them.\n\nActually, let me re-read the `character_expansion_positivity` lemma more carefully. It states:\n\nIf K(x, y) = ∑_i a_i · Φ_i(x) · conj(Φ_i(θ y)) with a_i ≥ 0 and θ measure-preserving (θ_*ν = μ), then:\n∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν dμ = ∑_i a_i · ‖∫ f · Φ_i dμ‖²\n\nThe key requirements are:\n1. K(x, y) = ∑_i a_i · Φ_i(x) · conj(Φ_i(θ y))\n2. θ : Y → X is measure-preserving (θ_*ν = μ)\n3. f is real-valued\n\nIn our case:\n- X = PosInterfaceConfig (the space of u = (U⁺, u⁰))\n- Y = positive configs (the space of V⁺)\n- θ(V⁺) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰))... but this depends on u⁰, which is part of x!\n\nSo obstruction 1 applies: θ depends on both x and y.\n\nBut wait — what if we reformulate? What if we take:\n- X = PosInterfaceConfig\n- Y = PosInterfaceConfig (the SAME space)\n- θ = σ (the σ reflection on interface configs)\n- K(u, v) = the full kernel\n\nThen the integral would be:\n∫∫ g(u) · g(σ(v)) · K(u, v) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nBut our integral is:\n∫_u g(u) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nThese are different. In our integral, the inner integral is over V⁺ only (not the full PosInterfaceConfig), and the σ is applied to u⁰ (part of u), not to v.\n\nHmm, but what if we change variables? Let v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)). Then the inner integral becomes:\n∫_v g(v) · K(u, v⁺) · δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)) dμ⁺⁰(v)\n\nThis is an integral over the full PosInterfaceConfig space, but with a δ constraint. This is NOT the standard form.\n\nOK, I think the key issue is that our integral is NOT of the form ∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν dμ. It's a different form because of the degenerate kernel.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Maybe I should NOT try to use `character_expansion_positivity` at all. Instead, I should prove the positivity directly from the character expansion.\n\nThe integral is:\nI = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nAfter the change of variables (step b):\nI = ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰))/2 + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nSubstituting g(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2):\nI = ∫_u f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(...))) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nNow, the full Boltzmann factor is:\nB(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n\nThis is a product of plaquette factors. By `plaquette_product_separable_decomp` (step a), the interface part decomposes as:\nexp(-β·S_int(...)) = ∑_w F(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n\nBut the S⁺ parts also need to be character-expanded. The S⁺(u) part is a product of positive plaquette factors, and S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is also a product of positive plaquette factors.\n\nSo the full Boltzmann factor B(u, V⁺) is a product of ALL plaquette factors (positive, interface, and reflected positive). By the character expansion of each plaquette factor and CG reduction, B(u, V⁺) decomposes as:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · ∏_l χ_{w(l)}(g_l)\n\nwhere the product is over ALL links (U⁺ links, u⁰ links, V⁺ links, and σ(u⁰) links — but σ(u⁰) links are u⁰ links with σ applied).\n\nWait, the σ(u⁰) links are the interface links with σ applied. In the character expansion, these appear as characters of σ(u⁰) links, which involve the dual map for time-like links.\n\nSo the full decomposition is:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nwhere:\n- Φ_w = product of characters over U⁺ links\n- Ψ_w = product of characters over u⁰ links (from S⁺(u) and S_int)\n- Ξ_w = product of characters over V⁺ links\n- Ω_w = product of characters over σ(u⁰) links (from S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) and S_int)\n\nNow, Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) involves characters of σ-reflected interface links. For time-like links, σ inverts them, so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g)) = χ_{dual}(g). For spatial links, σ keeps them, so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g).\n\nSo Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w'(l)}(u⁰_l) where w'(l) = dual(w(l)) for time-like links and w'(l) = w(l) for spatial links.\n\nThe u⁰-dependent part is Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w_1(l)}(u⁰_l) · χ_{w_2(l)}(u⁰_l) where w_1 comes from Ψ_w and w_2 comes from Ω_w (with dual for time-like).\n\nBy CG, this product reduces to a single character sum:\nΨ_w(u⁰) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∑_λ N^λ_w · χ_λ(u⁰)\n\nwith N^λ_w ≥ 0.\n\nSo:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_{w, λ} G(w) · N^λ_w · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · χ_λ(u⁰)\n\nNow, the integral is:\nI = ∑_{w, λ} G(w) · N^λ_w · ∫_{u⁰} χ_λ(u⁰) · [∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺)] · [∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺)] dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nDefine:\n- A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺)\n- B_w(u⁰) = ∫_{V⁺} f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nThen:\nI = ∑_{w, λ} G(w) · N^λ_w · ∫_{u⁰} χ_λ(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) · B_w(u⁰) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nNow, by the reflection symmetry, B_w(u⁰) = A_w(σ(u⁰)) (if Ξ_w = Φ_w, which follows from the symmetry of the character expansion).\n\nSo:\nI = ∑_{w, λ} G(w) · N^λ_w · ∫_{u⁰} χ_λ(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰)) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nThis is STILL obstruction 3. The integral ∫ χ_λ · A_w · A_w(σ) dμ⁰ is NOT character orthogonality.\n\nOK so I keep arriving at the same obstruction. Let me think about what's fundamentally different in the OS proof.\n\nActually, I think the key insight I'm missing is that the OS proof does NOT expand f (or g) in characters. Instead, it shows that the KERNEL of T is a positive-definite function, which implies T is a positive operator.\n\nThe kernel of T (as an operator on L²(μ⁺⁰)) is:\nK_T(u, v) = [the kernel such that (Tg)(u) = ∫ K_T(u, v) · g(v) dμ⁺⁰(v)]\n\nBut as I noted, T is NOT a standard integral operator because of the σ(u⁰) in the argument of g. So T doesn't have a standard kernel.\n\nWait, but maybe we can write T as a standard integral operator by absorbing the σ. Let me think...\n\n(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(V⁺)\n\nLet v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)). Then V⁺ = v⁺ and σ(u⁰) = v⁰, so u⁰ = σ⁻¹(v⁰) = σ(v⁰) (since σ is an involution). So:\n\n(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{v: v⁰ = σ(u⁰)} g(v) · exp(-β·(S⁺(v)/2 + S_int(U⁺, σ(v⁰), reflect(v⁺)))) dμ⁺(v⁺)\n\nThis is an integral over the submanifold v⁰ = σ(u⁰), which is a \"slice\" of the PosInterfaceConfig space. It's NOT a standard integral operator.\n\nTo make it a standard integral operator, we'd need to integrate over the FULL PosInterfaceConfig space. But the integral is only over V⁺ (with v⁰ fixed to σ(u⁰)).\n\nI think the key insight is that the OS proof does NOT treat T as a standard integral operator. Instead, it directly shows the quadratic form ⟨g, Tg⟩ is non-negative.\n\nAnd the way it does this is by expanding the FULL Boltzmann factor (including S⁺) in characters, and then using character orthogonality to evaluate the integrals over ALL link variables (U⁺, u⁰, V⁺).\n\nBut as I showed, the u⁰ integral involves A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰)) · χ_λ(u⁰), which is NOT character orthogonality.\n\nUNLESS... the OS proof expands f (or g) in characters too. If f(u⁺, u⁰) = ∑_μ c_μ · χ_μ(u⁺) · χ_ν(u⁰) (character expansion of f), then A_w(u⁰) = ∑_μ c_μ · [∫ χ_μ(u⁺) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺] · χ_ν(u⁰), and the u⁰ integral becomes a triple character integral, which CAN be evaluated using character orthogonality (or more precisely, using the CG coefficients).\n\nBut f is ARBITRARY. We can't assume f has a character expansion (well, by Peter-Weyl, every L² function does, but the expansion is infinite in general).\n\nHmm, but for a FINITE lattice, the link variables take values in SU(N), and L²(SU(N)^n) has a countable orthonormal basis of characters. The expansion of f in this basis is:\nf(u) = ∑_λ c_λ · χ_λ(u)\n\nwhere the sum is over all multi-indices λ = (λ_1, ..., λ_n) (one character per link). This is the Peter-Weyl theorem.\n\nIf we substitute this expansion into the integral, we get:\nI = ∑_{λ, μ} c_λ · c_μ · [integral involving χ_λ(u) · χ_μ(σ(u⁰), V⁺) · B(u, V⁺)]\n\nAnd by character orthogonality, the integral evaluates to δ_{λ, μ} (or something similar), giving:\nI = ∑_λ c_λ² · [non-negative constant] ≥ 0\n\nBut this requires the FULL Peter-Weyl theorem (not just the character expansion of the Boltzmann factor), and the expansion of f is infinite in general.\n\nFor a FINITE lattice, the number of links is finite, but each link variable is in SU(N), which is continuous. So L²(SU(N)^n) is infinite-dimensional, and the character expansion of f is an infinite sum.\n\nThis makes the formalization very difficult. We'd need to handle infinite sums and convergence.\n\nOK, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me take a completely practical approach.\n\nThe design doc §5 suggests the \"derived axiom\" approach. The idea is to decompose the problem into two cleaner pieces. Let me think about what derived axiom would work.\n\nThe key insight is that the derived axiom should capture the SEPARABLE DECOMPOSITION of the kernel, and the measure-theory part (step 2) should be pure measure theory + character orthogonality.\n\nBut as I showed, the separable decomposition K(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰))) leads to obstruction 3.\n\nSo maybe the derived axiom should be DIFFERENT. Maybe it should directly state the positivity, but in a form that can be proved from the character expansion.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `character_expansion_positivity` lemma. It requires:\n1. K(x, y) = ∑_i a_i · Φ_i(x) · conj(Φ_i(θ y))\n2. θ : Y → X is measure-preserving (θ_*ν = μ)\n3. f is real-valued\n\nAnd concludes: ∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν dμ = ∑_i a_i · ‖∫ f · Φ_i dμ‖² ≥ 0\n\nIn our case, the integral is:\nI = ∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nIf we set:\n- X = PosInterfaceConfig (space of u)\n- Y = positive configs (space of V⁺)\n- θ(V⁺) = ??? — this needs to be a function of V⁺ only, but σ(u⁰) depends on u⁰\n\nThe problem is that θ needs to map Y → X, but in our case, the \"reflected\" config (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) depends on u⁰ (part of x = u).\n\nBUT — what if we reformulate? What if we take:\n- X = PosInterfaceConfig\n- Y = PosInterfaceConfig (the SAME space)\n- θ = σ ∘ restrictToInterface (i.e., θ(v) = (v⁺, σ(v⁰)))\n- K(u, v) = the full kernel, but with v playing the role of the \"reflected\" config\n\nThen the integral would be:\n∫∫ g(u) · g(θ(v)) · K(u, v) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nBut our integral is:\n∫_u ∫_{V⁺} g(u) · g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · K(u, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nThese are different. In our integral, the inner integral is over V⁺ only (not the full PosInterfaceConfig), and σ is applied to u⁰ (part of u), not to v⁰.\n\nHmm, but what if we change variables in the inner integral? Let v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)). Then V⁺ = v⁺ and u⁰ = σ(v⁰) (since σ is an involution). The inner integral becomes:\n∫_{v: v⁰ = σ(u⁰)} g(v) · K(u, v⁺) dμ⁺(v⁺)\n\nThis is an integral over the \"slice\" v⁰ = σ(u⁰), not the full space.\n\nTo make it a full integral, we'd need to integrate over v⁰ too, but v⁰ is fixed to σ(u⁰).\n\nI think the fundamental issue is that our integral is NOT of the form ∫∫ f(x) · f(θ y) · K(x, y) dν dμ. It's a different form.\n\nOK, let me try yet another approach. What if the derived axiom is stated at the level of the integral, not the kernel?\n\n**Derived Axiom:** For the transfer matrix T (defined as transferMatrixReflected), there exists a finite index set W, non-negative coefficients a : W → ℝ, and measurable functions Φ : W → (PosInterfaceConfig → ℂ) such that for all real-valued g : PosInterfaceConfig → ℝ:\n```\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∑_w a_w · |∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)|²\n```\n\nThis directly implies positivity. And it can be proved from the character expansion + character orthogonality (the hard part).\n\nBut this derived axiom is essentially just \"T is a positive operator,\" which is what we're trying to prove. It's not more \"fundamental\" than transferMatrixPositivity_axiom.\n\nHowever, the advantage is that this derived axiom is PURELY about the transfer matrix structure, without reference to the reflection positivity setup. It can be proved from the character expansion (which is about the Boltzmann factor), while transferMatrixPositivity_axiom is about the reflection positivity integral.\n\nActually, I think the advantage of the derived axiom approach is that it separates the measure-theory part from the representation-theory part. The derived axiom captures the representation-theory part (character expansion + CG + character orthogonality), and the measure-theory part (showing the integral equals the sum of |Fourier coefficients|²) is pure measure theory.\n\nBut as I showed, the measure-theory part is NOT straightforward because of the σ reflection obstruction.\n\nOK, I think I need to stop analyzing and start doing. Let me take the following practical approach:\n\n1. First, let me understand the current code better by reading the key definitions.\n2. Then, let me think about what's the most tractable next step.\n3. Start implementing.\n\nThe most tractable approach, given the obstructions, is probably:\n\n**Option 1: Direct approach** — Try to prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom directly from the character expansion + character orthogonality. This requires overcoming obstruction 3.\n\n**Option 2: Derived axiom approach** — Axiomatize the key identity and prove positivity from it. This decomposes the problem but doesn't reduce the axiom count until step 3 is done.\n\n**Option 3: Strengthen the character expansion** — Maybe the character expansion needs to be done in a way that accounts for the σ reflection, giving a decomposition that directly leads to positivity.\n\nI think Option 3 is the key. The character expansion needs to be done in a way that the u⁰-dependent part is handled correctly.\n\nLet me think about this. The full Boltzmann factor is:\nB(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺))))\n\nBy the reflection symmetry, B(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) (swapping roles). This means B is symmetric under the swap (U⁺, u⁰) ↔ (V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nThe character expansion gives:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nBy the reflection symmetry:\n∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nThis means the decomposition is symmetric. In particular, if we relabel, we can write:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nWait, this would require Ξ_w = Φ_w and Ω_w = Ψ_w (with the σ reflection). Is this true?\n\nBy the reflection symmetry, the character expansion of B(u, V⁺) is the same as the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺). If the character expansion is unique (which it is, by linear independence of characters), then the coefficients must match.\n\nBut the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) is:\n∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. The character expansion of B(u, V⁺) treats U⁺, u⁰, V⁺, σ(u⁰) as the link variables. When we swap (U⁺, u⁰) ↔ (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), the link variables become V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, u⁰. So the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) treats V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, u⁰ as the link variables.\n\nIf the character expansion is:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nThen by the reflection symmetry:\nB(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nWait, but the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) would treat V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, σ(σ(u⁰)) = V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, u⁰ as the link variables. So:\nB(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nHmm, but this assumes the character expansion has the same structure when we swap the roles. This might not be the case because the plaquette structure is different.\n\nActually, I think the reflection symmetry means that the character expansion of B(u, V⁺) can be written as:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)))\n\nWait, why conj? Because the Boltzmann factor is real, so B(u, V⁺) = conj(B(u, V⁺)). And by the reflection symmetry, B(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺). So:\nB(u, V⁺) = conj(B(u, V⁺)) = conj(B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺))\n\nIf B(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)), then:\nconj(B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺)) = ∑_w G(w) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺)) · conj(Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))) · conj(Ξ_w(U⁺)) · conj(Ω_w(u⁰))\n\nFor B(u, V⁺) = conj(B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺)):\n∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∑_w G(w) · conj(Φ_w(V⁺)) · conj(Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))) · conj(Ξ_w(U⁺)) · conj(Ω_w(u⁰))\n\nThis means Ξ_w = conj(Φ_w) and Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = conj(Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)))... wait, that's not quite right. Let me think more carefully.\n\nActually, the reflection symmetry says B(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺), NOT B(u, V⁺) = conj(B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺)). The Boltzmann factor is real, so B = conj(B), but the reflection symmetry is a separate property.\n\nLet me be more careful. The reflection symmetry says:\nS⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(U⁺, u⁰, reflect(V⁺)) = S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S⁺(U⁺, u⁰) + S_int(V⁺, σ(u⁰), reflect(U⁺))\n\nWait, that's just the same thing with u and V⁺ swapped. So B(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) means the Boltzmann factor is symmetric under the swap (U⁺, u⁰) ↔ (V⁺, σ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, the character expansion of B(u, V⁺) is:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nAnd the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) is:\nB(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nWait, but the character expansion of B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) treats V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, σ(σ(u⁰)) = V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺, u⁰ as the link variables. So the expansion is:\nB(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nFor B(u, V⁺) = B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺):\n∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ξ_w(V⁺) · Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) · Ξ_w(U⁺) · Ω_w(u⁰)\n\nBy the linear independence of characters, we can match terms. This gives:\nΞ_w = Φ_w and Ω_w(σ(u⁰)) = Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))... no, that's not right either. The matching is more subtle because the link variables are different.\n\nActually, I think the matching gives:\n- The U⁺ links in B(u, V⁺) correspond to the U⁺ links in B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) (which are in the \"Ξ\" position).\n- The u⁰ links in B(u, V⁺) correspond to the u⁰ links in B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) (which are in the \"Ω\" position, because σ(σ(u⁰)) = u⁰).\n- The V⁺ links in B(u, V⁺) correspond to the V⁺ links in B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) (which are in the \"Φ\" position).\n- The σ(u⁰) links in B(u, V⁺) correspond to the σ(u⁰) links in B(V⁺, σ(u⁰), U⁺) (which are in the \"Ψ\" position).\n\nSo the matching gives:\nΦ_w(U⁺) = Ξ_w(U⁺) → Φ_w = Ξ_w\nΨ_w(u⁰) = Ω_w(u⁰) → Ψ_w = Ω_w\nΞ_w(V⁺) = Φ_w(V⁺) → Ξ_w = Φ_w\nΩ_w(σ(u⁰)) = Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) → Ω_w = Ψ_w\n\nSo Ξ_w = Φ_w and Ω_w = Ψ_w!\n\nThis means:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))\n\nNow, Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) involves characters of σ-reflected interface links. For time-like links, σ inverts them, so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g)) = χ_{dual}(g). For spatial links, σ keeps them, so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g).\n\nSo Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) = ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{σ*(w(l))}(u⁰_l) where σ*(w(l)) = dual(w(l)) for time-like links and σ*(w(l)) = w(l) for spatial links.\n\nTherefore:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Φ_w(V⁺) · Ψ_w(u⁰) · Ψ_w(σ(u⁰))\n= ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Φ_w(V⁺) · ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l) · χ_{σ*(w(l))}(u⁰_l)\n\nThe u⁰-dependent part is ∏_{l ∈ u⁰} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l) · χ_{σ*(w(l))}(u⁰_l).\n\nFor time-like links: χ_{w(l)}(g) · χ_{dual(w(l))}(g) = χ_{w(l)}(g) · conj(χ_{w(l)}(g)) = |χ_{w(l)}(g)|² ≥ 0!\n\nFor spatial links: χ_{w(l)}(g) · χ_{w(l)}(g) = χ_{w(l)}(g)², which is complex in general.\n\nWait, so for time-like links, the product is |χ|² ≥ 0, but for spatial links, the product is χ², which is complex. So the u⁰-dependent part is NOT necessarily real and non-negative.\n\nHmm, but the full Boltzmann factor B(u, V⁺) is real. So the imaginary parts must cancel. But the individual terms in the sum might be complex.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. For spatial interface links, σ keeps them (σ(g) = g), so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g). So Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) for spatial links is χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l) (same as Ψ_w(u⁰)). So the product is χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)² for spatial links.\n\nBut for time-like links, σ inverts them (σ(g) = g⁻¹), so χ(σ(g)) = χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g)) = χ_{dual}(g). So Ψ_w(σ(u⁰)) for time-like links is χ_{dual(w(l))}(u⁰_l) = conj(χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)). So the product is χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l) · conj(χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)) = |χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)|² ≥ 0.\n\nSo the u⁰-dependent part is:\n∏_{l ∈ u⁰_time} |χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)|² · ∏_{l ∈ u⁰_spatial} χ_{w(l)}(u⁰_l)²\n\nThe time-like part is non-negative, but the spatial part is complex. So the overall u⁰-dependent part is complex, and the individual terms in the sum are complex.\n\nBut the full Boltzmann factor is real, so the sum of all terms is real. The individual terms might be complex, but they cancel.\n\nThis means the decomposition B(u, V⁺) = ∑_w G(w) · Φ_w(U⁺) · Φ_w(V⁺) · [u⁰-dependent part] does NOT have non-negative individual terms (the u⁰-dependent part can be complex).\n\nSo this approach doesn't directly give positivity.\n\nHmm, but wait. For spatial interface links, σ(g) = g, so the character expansion of the S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) part for spatial links is the same as for S⁺(V⁺, u⁰). This means the spatial interface links appear in the SAME way in both S⁺(u) and S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)). So the character expansion for spatial interface links gives χ², which is complex.\n\nBut the OS proof must handle this. Let me think about how.\n\nActually, I think the key is that for spatial interface links, the character expansion gives χ², which can be reduced via CG to a sum of single characters with non-negative coefficients:\nχ_s(g) · χ_s(g) = ∑_w N^w_{s,s} · χ_w(g) with N^w_{s,s} ≥ 0\n\nSo after CG reduction, the spatial interface links also appear with single characters, and the u⁰-dependent part becomes a sum of single characters.\n\nBut then we're back to the same situation: the u⁰ integral involves A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰)) · χ_λ(u⁰), which is NOT character orthogonality.\n\nOK, I think I really need to look at the actual OS proof. Let me search for it.\n\nActually, let me think about this from a different angle. The OS proof shows T = B*·B. The operator B is defined via the character expansion. The key is that B extracts Fourier coefficients, and T = B*·B means the kernel of T is ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)).\n\nBut our T is NOT a standard integral operator. So T ≠ B*·B in the standard sense.\n\nHowever, the QUADRATIC FORM ⟨g, Tg⟩ can still be non-negative, even if T ≠ B*·B. The OS proof might show ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² directly, without showing T = B*·B.\n\nLet me think about what B is. If B is defined as:\n(Bg)(w) = ∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nthen ‖Bg‖² = ∑_w |(Bg)(w)|² = ∑_w |∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)|².\n\nAnd ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u).\n\nFor ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖², we need:\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∑_w |∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)|²\n\nThis is the key identity. If this holds, then ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0.\n\nNow, the RHS is:\n∑_w [∫_u g(u) · Φ_w(u) dμ⁺⁰(u)] · conj([∫_v g(v) · Φ_w(v) dμ⁺⁰(v)])\n= ∫_u ∫_v g(u) · g(v) · ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n= ∫_u ∫_v g(u) · g(v) · K_{B*B}(u, v) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nwhere K_{B*B}(u, v) = ∑_w Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)).\n\nAnd the LHS is:\n∫_u g(u) · (Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nFor LHS = RHS, we need:\n∫_u g(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫_{V⁺} g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u) = ∫_u ∫_v g(u) · g(v) · K_{B*B}(u, v) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nThe LHS has g(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) (with σ(u⁰) determined by u⁰), while the RHS has g(v) (with v free). These are different unless we can relate them.\n\nThe key insight: on the LHS, the second g is evaluated at (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), which is a PosInterfaceConfig. On the RHS, the second g is evaluated at v, which is also a PosInterfaceConfig. If we set v = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), then the LHS becomes:\n∫_u ∫_{v: v⁰ = σ(u⁰)} g(u) · g(v) · exp(-β·(...)) dμ⁺(v⁺) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nAnd the RHS is:\n∫_u ∫_v g(u) · g(v) · K_{B*B}(u, v) dμ⁺⁰(v) dμ⁺⁰(u)\n\nFor these to be equal, we need:\nexp(-β·(...)) · δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)) · [Jacobian] = K_{B*B}(u, v)\n\nBut the LHS has a δ(v⁰ = σ(u⁰)), which is a distribution, while the RHS is a function. These can't be equal unless the δ is somehow absorbed.\n\nI think the issue is that the LHS integral is over a \"slice\" (v⁰ = σ(u⁰)), while the RHS integral is over the full space. These are fundamentally different.\n\nSo ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≠ ‖Bg‖² in general. The OS proof must use a different argument.\n\nOK, I've spent a LOT of time on this analysis. Let me take a completely different approach. Instead of trying to understand the full OS proof, let me focus on what's tractable for formalization.\n\nThe most tractable approach is probably:\n\n1. **State a derived axiom** that captures the key identity (whatever form it takes).\n2. **Prove transferMatrixPositivity_axiom from the derived axiom** (this should be straightforward measure theory).\n3. **Prove the derived axiom from peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette + characterOrthogonality** (this is the hard part, but it's pure representation theory).\n\nFor step 1, the derived axiom should be something that:\n- Implies the positivity of the transfer matrix.\n- Can be proved from the character expansion + character orthogonality.\n\nThe simplest derived axiom is just: \"The transfer matrix T is positive,\" i.e., ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0 for all g. But this is exactly what we're trying to prove, so it's not helpful.\n\nA more structured derived axiom would be: \"The kernel of T has a separable decomposition of the form ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(v)) with a_w ≥ 0.\" But as I showed, T is NOT a standard integral operator, so it doesn't have a kernel of this form.\n\nHmm, let me think about this differently. Maybe the derived axiom should be about the BOLTZMANN FACTOR, not the transfer matrix.\n\n**Derived Axiom:** The full Boltzmann factor B(u, V⁺) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u) + S⁺(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) + S_int(...))) has a character expansion of the form:\nB(u, V⁺) = ∑_w a_w · Φ_w(u) · conj(Φ_w(θ(V⁺, u⁰)))\nwhere θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), a_w ≥ 0.\n\nIf this holds, then by `character_expansion_positivity` (with appropriate modifications for the x-dependent θ), the integral is ≥ 0.\n\nBut as I showed, this leads to obstruction 3. So this CAN'T be the right derived axiom.\n\nUNLESS... the `character_expansion_positivity` lemma can be generalized to handle the x-dependent θ. Let me think about this.\n\nThe `character_expansion_positivity` lemma requires θ : Y → X (a function of y only). In our case, θ(V⁺, u⁰) depends on u⁰ (part of x). So the lemma doesn't directly apply.\n\nBut maybe we can generalize the lemma. The key step in the proof is the change of variables z = θ(y), which requires θ to be a function of y only. If θ depends on x, the change of variables gives a result that depends on x, preventing the factorization.\n\nHowever, in our case, θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)). The u⁰ dependence is only in the interface part. If we split the integral ∫_u = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺}, then for fixed u⁰, θ(V⁺, u⁰) = (V⁺, σ(u⁰)) is a function of V⁺ only (since u⁰ is fixed). So we can apply the lemma for each fixed u⁰.\n\nBut the issue is that the lemma requires θ to be measure-preserving from ν (on Y) to μ (on X). In our case, for fixed u⁰, θ(·, u⁰) maps V⁺ to (V⁺, σ(u⁰)), which is a map from positive configs to PosInterfaceConfigs. The pushforward of μ⁺ by this map is μ⁺ × δ_{σ(u⁰)}, which is NOT μ⁺⁰.\n\nSo the lemma doesn't apply even for fixed u⁰.\n\nOK, I think the fundamental issue is that the `character_expansion_positivity` lemma is NOT the right scaffold for the lattice case. The design doc and gap_analysis.md both confirm this.\n\nLet me think about what the right scaffold is. The OS proof shows positivity by a different argument. Let me try to understand it by looking at the actual proof.\n\nActually, let me search for the Osterwalder-Seiler proof or the Lüscher proof.\n\nLet me search the literature.\n\nActually, I realize I should just search for the key mathematical fact. Let me search for \"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity transfer matrix character expansion\" or similar.\n\nLet me do a web search.\n\nActually, let me first check if there are any relevant papers in the literature/ directory.\n\nLet me also re-read the key definitions in the code to understand the exact structure.\n\nOK, I think I've spent enough time analyzing. Let me take a practical approach:\n\n1. Search the literature for the OS proof structure.\n2. Read the key code definitions.\n3. Decide on a strategy and start implementing.\n\nLet me start by searching the literature and reading the code.\n\nActually, I realize I should focus on what's actionable. Let me:\n\n1. Read the key definitions in TransferMatrix.lean (g_posInterface, transferMatrixCorrect, integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg)\n2. Search for the OS proof in the l