 σ. So if the CG decomposition handles the interface time-like links, the remaining integral over the other links (positive links and interface spatial links) doesn't involve σ.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. After the character expansion and CG decomposition, the interface time-like links appear with single characters. The integral over the interface time-like links is:\n∫_{g ∈ interface time-like} f(u⁺, ..., g, ...) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) = c_w(u⁺, ..., interface spatial links, V⁺, ...)\n\nThis is a Fourier coefficient of f as a function of the interface time-like links, with other variables fixed. It's a function of the positive links, interface spatial links, and V⁺.\n\nNow, the remaining integral is over the positive links, interface spatial links, and V⁺. The σ reflection doesn't affect these (σ only inverts interface time-like links). So the remaining integral is:\n∫_{positive, spatial interface, V⁺} c_w(...) · conj(c_w(...)) dμ = ∫ |c_w|² dμ ≥ 0\n\nWait, is this right? Let me check.\n\nThe integral I is:\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · B(u⁺, u⁰, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nAfter the character expansion of B and CG decomposition, the interface time-like links (part of u⁰) appear with single characters. The integral over the interface time-like links is:\n∫_{g ∈ u⁰_time} f(u⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · f(V⁺, σ(u⁰_spatial), σ(g)) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)\n\nWait, but f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) = f(V⁺, σ(u⁰_spatial), σ(g)). And σ(g) = g⁻¹ for time-like links. So:\nf(V⁺, σ(u⁰_spatial), σ(g)) = f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) [since σ keeps spatial links and inverts time-like links]\n\nSo the integral over g is:\n∫_g f(u⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)\n\nThis is NOT a standard Fourier coefficient. It involves f at both g and g⁻¹.\n\nHmm, but by the change of variables g → g⁻¹ (which is measure-preserving):\n∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) = ∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g⁻¹) dμ(g) = ∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · conj(χ_w(g)) dμ(g) = conj(∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g) dμ(g))\n\nWait, that's only true if f is real-valued. Let me check. f is real-valued (it's a function to ℝ). So:\n∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) = ∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g⁻¹) dμ(g) [by change of variables g → g⁻¹]\n= ∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · conj(χ_w(g)) dμ(g) [since χ_w(g⁻¹) = conj(χ_w(g))]\n= conj(∫_g conj(f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g)) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)) [since conj of integral = integral of conj]\n= conj(∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)) [since f is real-valued, conj(f) = f]\n\nWait, that's not right. Let me be more careful.\n\n∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)\n\nBy the change of variables h = g⁻¹ (measure-preserving):\n= ∫_h f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, h) · χ_w(h⁻¹) dμ(h)\n= ∫_h f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, h) · conj(χ_w(h)) dμ(h) [since χ_w(h⁻¹) = conj(χ_w(h))]\n\nNow, since f is real-valued:\n= ∫_h f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, h) · conj(χ_w(h)) dμ(h)\n= conj(∫_h conj(f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, h)) · χ_w(h) dμ(h)) [conj of integral]\n= conj(∫_h f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, h) · χ_w(h) dμ(h)) [since f is real-valued]\n\nSo:\n∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) = conj(∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g) dμ(g))\n\nLet c_w(u⁺, u⁰_spatial) = ∫_g f(u⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) [Fourier coefficient of f w.r.t. g]\nLet d_w(V⁺, u⁰_spatial) = ∫_g f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · χ_w(g) dμ(g) [Fourier coefficient of f w.r.t. g, with V⁺ instead of u⁺]\n\nNote that d_w(V⁺, u⁰_spatial) = c_w(V⁺, u⁰_spatial) (just relabeling u⁺ → V⁺).\n\nSo the integral over g is:\n∫_g f(u⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)\n\nHmm, this is NOT simply c_w · conj(d_w). It's a triple integral: ∫ f(u⁺, g) · f(V⁺, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g). This is NOT a product of two separate integrals.\n\nWait, I think I made an error. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe integral I is:\nI = ∫_{u⁰} ∫_{u⁺} ∫_{V⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · B(u⁺, u⁰, V⁺) dμ⁺(V⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺) dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nAfter the character expansion of B, the interface time-like links (part of u⁰) appear with single characters. But the integral over u⁰ involves BOTH f(u⁺, u⁰) and f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)), which both depend on u⁰. So the integral over u⁰ is NOT separable into a product of two integrals.\n\nThe integral over u⁰ is:\n∫_{u⁰} f(u⁺, u⁰) · f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)) · [u⁰-dependent part of B] dμ⁰(u⁰)\n\nThis is a single integral over u⁰, involving both f(u⁺, u⁰) and f(V⁺, σ(u⁰)). It's NOT separable.\n\nSo the integral over the interface time-like links is:\n∫_g f(u⁺, u⁰_spatial, g) · f(V⁺, u⁰_spatial, g⁻¹) · χ_w(g) dμ(g)\n\nThis is a \"triple product\" integral, NOT a product of two separate integrals. So it's NOT |Fourier coefficient|².\n\nHmm, but the OS proof says T = B*·B, which gives ⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖². So the integral MUST be a sum of |Fourier coefficients|². How?\n\nI think the key is that the character expansion is done differently. Instead of expanding B and then integrating, the OS proof might expand the ENTIRE integrand (including f) in characters.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is that the OS proof does NOT expand B alone. It expands the PRODUCT f(u) · B(u, V⁺) in characters. Since f is arbitrary, this expansion involves the Fourier coefficients of f.\n\nWait, but f is the test function, not the Boltzmann factor. The character expansion of f is the Peter-Weyl expansion, which is an infinite sum in general.\n\nOK, I think I'm stuck on the mathematical details. Let me take a completely different approach.\n\nLet me search the web for the actual proof. I'll search for \"Lüscher transfer matrix positivity\" or \"Osterwalder Seiler reflection positivity proof\".\n\nActually, let me look at the literature/ directory first.","turn":20,"is_prune_marker":false}
