{"id":36,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\literature\\survey.md\n-- 126 lines, 8015 bytes --\n  1 | # Literature Survey: Yang-Mills Existence and Mass Gap\n  2 | \n  3 | ## Overview\n  4 | \n  5 | The Yang-Mills Millennium Prize problem asks for a rigorous construction of\n  6 | quantum Yang-Mills theory on ℝ⁴ satisfying the Wightman (or Osterwalder-Schrader)\n  7 | axioms with a positive mass gap. This survey maps the current state of rigorous\n  8 | results, active programs, and documented challenges.\n  9 | \n 10 | ---\n 11 | \n 12 | ## 1. Established Rigorous Results\n 13 | \n 14 | ### 1.1 Two-Dimensional Yang-Mills (Solved)\n 15 | - **2D YM is exactly solvable**: The Migdal-Witten approach gives explicit formulas for Wilson loop expectations.\n 16 | - **Stochastic quantization**: Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen (2022) constructed the 2D Yang-Mills measure via stochastic Langevin dynamic, establishing a Markov process on a space of distributional connections.\n 17 |   - *Key ref*: \"Langevin dynamic for the 2D Yang-Mills measure\" (arXiv, 2022)\n 18 | - **Mass gap in 2D**: YM on ℝ² has no propagating degrees of freedom (topological theory), so mass gap is trivial.\n 19 | \n 20 | ### 1.2 Three-Dimensional Yang-Mills (Largely Solved)\n 21 | - **Balaban's RG for 3D YM**: First rigorous construction using renormalization group methods on the lattice, taking the continuum limit.\n 22 | - **Magnen-Seneor-Rivasseau**: Constructive approach using cluster expansions.\n 23 | - **Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen (2023)**: Extended stochastic quantization to Yang-Mills-Higgs in 3D, constructing a state space and Markov process for the stochastic quantisation equation.\n 24 |   - *Key ref*: \"Stochastic quantisation of Yang-Mills-Higgs in 3D\" (arXiv, 2023)\n 25 | - **Mass gap in 3D**: Established — exponential decay of correlations proven.\n 26 | \n 27 | ### 1.3 Four-Dimensional Yang-Mills (Open Problem)\n 28 | - **Renormalizability**: Proven by 't Hooft, Veltman, and others (awarded Nobel Prize). Pure YM is renormalizable in 4D.\n 29 | - **Perturbative asymptotic freedom**: Gross, Wilczek, Politzer (Nobel Prize) — the coupling constant decreases at high energies, implying UV completeness perturbatively.\n 30 | - **No rigorous non-perturbative construction exists** for pure YM in 4D.\n 31 | \n 32 | ---\n 33 | \n 34 | ## 2. Active Research Programs\n 35 | \n 36 | ### 2.1 Balaban's Renormalization Group Program\n 37 | - **Approach**: Lattice gauge theory + rigorous RG transformations. Block spin renormalization group on lattice YM.\n 38 | - **Status**: Most developed rigorous framework for non-Abelian gauge theories.\n 39 |   - ✓ 3D YM constructed\n 40 |   - ✓ 4D YM-Higgs (with scalar field) constructed — the Higgs mechanism provides a mass scale\n 41 |   - ✗ 4D pure YM — incomplete. The RG flow for pure YM in 4D hits the \"strong coupling problem\" in the infrared\n 42 | - **Key challenge**: Controlling the IR behavior of the RG flow without a Higgs field or mass term.\n 43 | \n 44 | ### 2.2 Stochastic Quantization (Hairer-Chandra-Shen)\n 45 | - **Approach**: Parisi-Wu stochastic quantization — study YM via an SPDE (Yang-Mills heat equation with noise). Use Hairer's regularity structures to handle the singular SPDE.\n 46 | - **Status**:\n 47 |   - ✓ 2D YM constructed via Langevin dynamic (Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen, 2022)\n 48 |   - ✓ 3D YM-Higgs constructed (Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen, 2023)\n 49 |   - ✗ 3D pure YM and 4D pure YM — open. The SPDE becomes more singular, requiring higher-order regularity structures.\n 50 | - **Strengths**: Elegant probabilistic framework. Bypasses lattice discretization. Direct connection to Euclidean QFT.\n 51 | - **Key challenge**: The YM SPDE in 4D has worse UV behavior. Need to handle non-linearities at higher regularity.\n 52 | \n 53 | ### 2.3 Probabilistic / Large-N (Chatterjee)\n 54 | - **Approach**: Study lattice YM on finite graphs using probabilistic methods. Large-N limit where the theory simplifies (planar diagrams dominate).\n 55 | - **Key result**: Chatterjee (2019+) proved that in the large-N limit, lattice YM converges to the \"master field\" describing a deterministic large-N theory.\n 56 | - **Status**: Significant progress on large-N lattice YM, but the continuum limit and mass gap in 4D at finite N remain open.\n 57 | \n 58 | ### 2.4 Geometric Analysis (Douglas-Henry, Mondal)\n 59 | - **Approach**: Study YM functional on the space of connections modulo gauge (orbit space). Equip orbit space with a natural Riemannian metric.\n 60 | - **Mondal (2023)**: \"A Geometric Approach to the Yang-Mills Mass Gap\" — proposes using orbit space geometry to obtain a positive mass gap.\n 61 | - **Status**: Early stage. Interesting geometric insights but no full construction yet.\n 62 | \n 63 | ### 2.5 Constructive QFT via Cluster Expansions (Magnen-Rivasseau-Seiler)\n 64 | - **Approach**: Traditional Euclidean constructive QFT methods — correlation inequalities, cluster expansions, Mayer expansions.\n 65 | - **Status**: Applied to YM in 3D, some work on 4D YM-Higgs. Pure 4D YM remains open.\n 66 | \n 67 | ---\n 68 | \n 69 | ## 3. Formalization Efforts (Proof Assistants)\n 70 | \n 71 | ### 3.1 QFT in Proof Assistants\n 72 | - **No existing formalization** of Wightman or OS axioms found in Lean 4 or Coq.\n 73 | - **Lean-Quantum** (Kasaura et al., 2024): Lean 4 library for quantum information theory (entropies, data processing inequality). Not QFT.\n 74 | - **Related formalization work**:\n 75 |   - Coq: Gödels incompleteness theorem (O'Connor), modal logics\n 76 |   - Lean 4: Ramanujan-Nagell theorem, Nagata's factoriality theorem\n 77 |   - Isabelle/HOL: Some analysis foundations\n 78 | \n 79 | ### 3.2 Analysis Foundations Available in Mathlib4\n 80 | - Functional analysis (Hilbert spaces, bounded operators)\n 81 | - Measure theory and probability\n 82 | - Differential geometry (manifolds, connections, principal bundles)\n 83 | - Lie groups and Lie algebras\n 84 | - **Missing**: QFT-specific structures (Wightman fields, OS axioms, Feynman integrals, renormalization)\n 85 | \n 86 | ---\n 87 | \n 88 | ## 4. Documented Failure Modes\n 89 | \n 90 | 1. **Pure YM in 4D has no mass scale**: Unlike YM-Higgs, pure YM has no intrinsic mass parameter. The mass gap must emerge dynamically from dimensional transmutation (asymptotic freedom generates Λ_QCD). This makes rigorous RG control extremely difficult.\n 91 | \n 92 | 2. **Infrared problem**: Even if UV is controlled (asymptotic freedom), the IR behavior of 4D YM involves strong coupling and confinement, which is non-perturbative.\n 93 | \n 94 | 3. **Gauge redundancy**: All approaches must handle gauge-fixing modulo Gribov copies. The orbit space (connections modulo gauge) has a complicated topology.\n 95 | \n 96 | 4. **Singular SPDE**: In stochastic quantization, the 4D YM SPDE is critical/supercritical for regularity structures — the noise is too rough relative to the nonlinearity.\n 97 | \n 98 | 5. **Osterwalder-Schrader positivity**: Even if a candidate measure is constructed on the lattice, proving reflection positivity and taking the continuum limit while preserving OS axioms is highly non-trivial.\n 99 | \n100 | ---\n101 | \n102 | ## 5. Summary and Implications for Strategy\n103 | \n104 | | Dimension | Status | Assumed Difficulty |\n105 | |-----------|--------|-------------------|\n106 | | 2D YM | Solved (exactly, Migdal-Witten) | Easy |\n107 | | 2D YM (stochastic) | Solved (Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen) | Medium |\n108 | | 3D YM | Largely solved (Balaban, Magnen-Seneor) | Hard |\n109 | | 3D YM (stochastic) | Solved with Higgs (Chandra-Chevyrev-Hairer-Shen) | Hard |\n110 | | 4D YM-Higgs | Constructed (Balaban, etc.) | Very Hard |\n111 | | **4D Pure YM** | **Open** | **Extremely Hard** |\n112 | \n113 | ### Most Promising Approaches (for 4D pure YM)\n114 | \n115 | 1. **Stochastic quantization + regularity structures**: The most active modern program. Already succeeded in 2D and 3D (with Higgs). May extend to 4D with higher-order regularity structures and renormalization.\n116 | \n117 | 2. **Balaban's RG on the lattice**: Deepest existing rigorous framework. Needs a breakthrough on the IR RG flow for pure YM.\n118 | \n119 | 3. **Geometric analysis (orbit space methods)**: Novel angle that may bypass some difficulties. Least developed.\n120 | \n121 | ### Recommendation for This Project\n122 | \n123 | Given the extreme difficulty of the pure 4D YM problem, a pragmatic strategy is:\n124 | - **Primary**: Formalize the known 2D and 3D results in Lean 4 first, building the mathematical infrastructure (Wightman/OS axioms, gauge theory).\n125 | - **Secondary**: Investigate whether the stochastic quantization approach (Hairer-Chandra-Shen) can be advanced toward 4D, starting from their 2D/3D results.\n126 | - **Tertiary**: Explore whether orbit space geometric methods (Mondal) can be combined with the constructive QFT framework.\n","timestamp":1785491179,"token_count":2754,"tool_call_id":"0d225242-5bf0-4d6f-a103-fee5b9f3b1f6","full_token_estimate":1808,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"literature/survey.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":126,"byte_count":8015,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":3,"edit_line":null},"turn":21,"is_prune_marker":false}
