{"id":59,"role":"Tool","content":"C:\\github\\YangMills\\README.md\n-- 748 lines, 49206 bytes --\n145 | \n146 | **Conclusion:** `character_expansion_positivity` is NOT the right scaffold for\n147 | the lattice case.  The correct approach (from the Osterwalder–Seiler proof) is\n148 | the **operator-theoretic** argument: show the transfer matrix `T = B* · B` for\n149 | some operator `B` defined via the character expansion (Peter–Weyl + CG), then\n150 | `⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.  This requires (a) a Clebsch–Gordan axiom for products\n151 | of characters of the same group element, and (b) the full combinatorial wiring\n152 | of the interface plaquette expansion.  See `docs/gap_analysis.md` §\"Precise\n153 | analysis of why `character_expansion_positivity` does NOT directly apply\" for\n154 | the full analysis.\n155 | \n156 | **Progress (2026-07-03 session):** Step (a) is now complete.  The\n157 | `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom has been **strengthened** to also\n158 | provide the Clebsch–Gordan decomposition for character products:\n159 | `χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w(g)` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (Littlewood–\n160 | Richardson).  This is the \"across-plaquette\" CG that was identified as the key\n161 | missing ingredient — it allows combining character expansions when the same\n162 | link variable appears in multiple plaquettes.  Two new lemmas proved from the\n163 | strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`):\n164 | `charProduct_PD` (product of two chars is PD via CG) and\n165 | `charProduct_finset_decomp` (finite product of chars of the same element\n166 | decomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters via iterated\n167 | CG).  Two further lemmas proved from the strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0\n168 | custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`): `charSum_product_decomp`\n169 | (product of two non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\n170 | non-negative-weighted char sum via CG) and `charSum_finprod_decomp` (finite\n171 | product of non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\n172 | non-negative-weighted char sum via iterated CG).  The axiom count remains\n173 | **six** (the strengthening enriches an existing axiom, it does not add a new\n174 | one).  Step (b) — formalizing the operator `B` and showing `T = B* · B` —\n175 | remains the major formalization effort.\n176 | \n177 | ## What is actually proved (no sorry, no axiom)\n178 | \n179 | ### SU(N) and general algebra\n180 | - `CompactSpace (SU N)`, `SecondCountableTopology (SU N)`\n181 | - `trace_commutator_zero`, `trace_mul_rotate`, `trace_unitary_conj_invariant`,\n182 |   `trace_sq_invariant`, `jacobi_identity`\n183 | - `adjointAction_preserves_trace_sq`, `adjointAction_preserves_skewHermitian`,\n184 |   `adjointAction_preserves_traceless`\n185 | - `lagrangian_gauge_invariant`\n186 | \n187 | ### Positive-definite function algebra\n188 | - `PositiveDefinite.add`, `.smul_nonneg`, `.conj_inv`, `.zero`, `.one`\n189 | - `PositiveDefinite.mul` (Schur product theorem / Hadamard product of PSD\n190 |   matrices)\n191 | - `PositiveDefinite.pow`, `.tendsto`, `.prod`, `.sum`, `.sum'`\n192 | - `repCharacter_positiveDefinite`, `fundamentalCharacter_positiveDefinite`,\n193 |   `reTrace_positiveDefinite`\n194 | - `IsUnitaryRepresentation`, `repCharacter`, `repCharacter_inv`\n195 |   (`χ(g⁻¹) = conj(χ(g))`)\n196 | - `IsIrreducible` — a unitary representation is irreducible if the only\n197 |   invariant subspaces are `{0}` and the whole space\n198 | - `characterOrthogonality` (axiom) — Schur orthogonality for irreducible\n199 |   unitary representations of a compact group: `∫ χ_λ·conj(χ_μ) dμ = δ_{λμ}`\n200 |   for normalized Haar measure.  Not in Mathlib; the key ingredient for the\n201 |   `|Fourier coefficient|²` decomposition of the reflection-positivity integral.\n202 | - `exp_reTrace_positiveDefinite` (single-link Boltzmann factor; proved\n203 |   unconditionally via the power-series / `PositiveDefinite.tendsto` argument —\n204 |   no axiom)\n\n... 544 more line(s) below (use offset=205 to continue reading)","timestamp":1785493680,"token_count":1250,"tool_call_id":"7a80c535-9d56-4f19-b0ce-cfdc7f520175","full_token_estimate":832,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"README.md","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":748,"byte_count":49206,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":43,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":60,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1785493975,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"old_text\":\"**Conclusion:** `character_expansion_positivity` is NOT the right scaffold for\\nthe lattice case.  The correct approach (from the Osterwalder–Seiler proof) is\\nthe **operator-theoretic** argument: show the transfer matrix `T = B* · B` for\\nsome operator `B` defined via the character expansion (Peter–Weyl + CG), then\\n`⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.  This requires (a) a Clebsch–Gordan axiom for products\\nof characters of the same group element, and (b) the full combinatorial wiring\\nof the interface plaquette expansion.  See `docs/gap_analysis.md` §\\\"Precise\\nanalysis of why `character_expansion_positivity` does NOT directly apply\\\" for\\nthe full analysis.\\n\\n**Progress (2026-07-03 session):** Step (a) is now complete.  The\\n`peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom has been **strengthened** to also\\nprovide the Clebsch–Gordan decomposition for character products:\\n`χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w(g)` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (Littlewood–\\nRichardson).  This is the \\\"across-plaquette\\\" CG that was identified as the key\\nmissing ingredient — it allows combining character expansions when the same\\nlink variable appears in multiple plaquettes.  Two new lemmas proved from the\\nstrengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`):\\n`charProduct_PD` (product of two chars is PD via CG) and\\n`charProduct_finset_decomp` (finite product of chars of the same element\\ndecomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters via iterated\\nCG).  Two further lemmas proved from the strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0\\ncustom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`): `charSum_product_decomp`\\n(product of two non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\\nnon-negative-weighted char sum via CG) and `charSum_finprod_decomp` (finite\\nproduct of non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\\nnon-negative-weighted char sum via iterated CG).  The axiom count remains\\n**six** (the strengthening enriches an existing axiom, it does not add a new\\none).  Step (b) — formalizing the operator `B` and showing `T = B* · B` —\\nremains the major formalization effort.\",\"new_text\":\"**Conclusion:** `character_expansion_positivity` is NOT the right scaffold for\\nthe lattice case.  The correct approach (from the Osterwalder–Seiler proof) is\\nthe **operator-theoretic** argument: show the transfer matrix `T = B* · B` for\\nsome operator `B` defined via the character expansion (Peter–Weyl + CG), then\\n`⟨g, Tg⟩ = ‖Bg‖² ≥ 0`.  This requires (a) a Clebsch–Gordan axiom for products\\nof characters of the same group element, and (b) the full combinatorial wiring\\nof the interface plaquette expansion.  See `docs/gap_analysis.md` §\\\"Precise\\nanalysis of why `character_expansion_positivity` does NOT directly apply\\\" for\\nthe full analysis.\\n\\n**Progress (2026-07-03 session):** Step (a) is now complete.  The\\n`peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` axiom has been **strengthened** to also\\nprovide the Clebsch–Gordan decomposition for character products:\\n`χ_s(g)·χ_t(g) = ∑_w cg s t w · χ_w(g)` with `cg s t w ≥ 0` (Littlewood–\\nRichardson).  This is the \\\"across-plaquette\\\" CG that was identified as the key\\nmissing ingredient — it allows combining character expansions when the same\\nlink variable appears in multiple plaquettes.  Two new lemmas proved from the\\nstrengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0 custom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`):\\n`charProduct_PD` (product of two chars is PD via CG) and\\n`charProduct_finset_decomp` (finite product of chars of the same element\\ndecomposes as a non-negative-weighted sum of single characters via iterated\\nCG).  Two further lemmas proved from the strengthened axiom (0 sorries, 0\\ncustom axioms — verified by `#print axioms`): `charSum_product_decomp`\\n(product of two non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\\nnon-negative-weighted char sum via CG) and `charSum_finprod_decomp` (finite\\nproduct of non-negative-weighted char sums decomposes as a\\nnon-negative-weighted char sum via iterated CG).  The axiom count remains\\n**six** (the strengthening enriches an existing axiom, it does not add a new\\none).  Step (b) — formalizing the operator `B` and showing `T = B* · B` —\\nremains the major formalization effort.\\n\\n**Step (c) analysis (2026-07-31 session): L² expansion obstruction.**  A\\ndetailed analysis of step (c) (using CG + character orthogonality to evaluate\\nthe integrals) revealed a **fundamental obstruction**: closing\\n`transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` from the current axioms alone is NOT\\npossible.  After steps (a)–(b), the integral becomes\\n`∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰)) dμ⁰(u⁰)` where\\n`A_w(u⁰) = ∫_{u⁺} f(u⁺, u⁰) · Φ_w(u⁺) dμ⁺(u⁺)` depends on the arbitrary\\ntest function `f`.  This is obstruction 3 (the `σ` reflection gives\\n`A_w(u⁰) · A_w(σ(u⁰))` instead of `|A_w(u⁰)|²`), and evaluating the `u⁰`\\nintegral requires expanding `A_w(u⁰)` in the **L² basis** (Peter–Weyl\\ncompleteness: matrix elements of irreducible representations span `L²(G)`),\\nwhich is NOT provided by the current axioms.  `peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette`\\nprovides the character expansion of the **Boltzmann factor** (a specific\\nfunction), not the L² expansion of **arbitrary** functions; and\\n`characterOrthogonality` provides orthogonality of characters, not\\ncompleteness.  The proposed path forward is to **strengthen**\\n`peterWeyl_clebschGordan_plaquette` to include the L² expansion and Schur\\northogonality of matrix elements (keeping the axiom count at 6), which would\\nthen allow closing `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` (count → 5).  See\\n`docs/transfer_matrix_positivity_design.md` §5a and `docs/gap_analysis.md`\\n§\\\"Step (c) analysis complete\\\" for the full analysis.\"}","name":"patch_file"},"id":"117ab577-de56-4d86-97fa-1b15880ba5dc","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1270,"reasoning_content":"Let me now document my key finding in the design doc. The finding is that closing transferMatrixPositivity_axiom requires the full Peter-Weyl theorem (L² expansion), not just the character expansion of the Boltzmann factor.\n\nLet me add a new section to the design doc documenting this finding, then update the gap analysis, and hand off.","turn":46,"is_prune_marker":false}
